## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## docs-refresh  (v2 — one agent aligns the docs in its natural
##                flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a DOC-ALIGNMENT campaign: re-read the repo's
## documentation against the actual current state of the code, fix the
## DOCUMENTATION (never the code), and land each aligned doc as a
## semantic `docs(scope):` commit in stride. When a repo has NO docs in
## scope yet, an authoring pass bootstraps an initial set first
## (DEFAULT-CREATE, unchanged from v0.3.0).
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` / `branch_improve_loop` v2: SAME core
## (one adaptive `campaign` agent + deterministic gates + a bounded
## continuation loop + git-as-state), with Doki's DETERMINISTIC AUDIT
## MACHINERY kept fully in front of it — the machinery IS the value the
## engine uniquely adds here:
##   scan_docs         — immutable doc-footprint enumeration + inter-run
##                       cache pre-verification (unchanged).
##   scan_code_surface — opt-in CLI/diagnostic surface inventory
##                       (unchanged; empty globs = disabled).
##   build_manifest    — deterministic anchor extraction + mechanical
##                       verification; emits the bounded drift_candidates
##                       working set (severity-sorted, doc-chunked — the
##                       v0.15/0.16 context-bounding lessons live here),
##                       the anchor-level coverage_pct, and (v2) the
##                       mechanically VERIFIED `doc::anchor` pairs that
##                       feed the inter-run cache.
##   campaign          — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools).
##                       Mission = adjudicate + fix the manifest's drift
##                       candidates, one doc at a time, committing each
##                       aligned doc in stride. Docs follow code; the
##                       writeable set is `.md` (+ Go comments when
##                       opted in); is_code_bug is the escape valve.
##                       git IS the durable state — a re-dispatch re-runs
##                       the manifest and the campaign continues from the
##                       commits earlier passes banked.
##   scope_check       — deterministic detector (v2 rewrite of v1's
##                       enforce_fix_scope): diffs the RUN BASE against
##                       the tree and fails the gate when anything
##                       outside the doc writeable-set changed. With
##                       git-as-state the campaign commits in stride, so
##                       out-of-scope edits are DETECTED and bounced back
##                       (the campaign must revert them) instead of
##                       silently checkout-reverted from a dirty tree.
##   verify_build      — the stack-agnostic build/test gate shared with
##     + verify_run      the other v2 bots: docs-only changes should
##                       never break a build, and when go_comment_globs
##                       opts Go comments in, the gate proves the tree
##                       still compiles. Deterministic exit code.
##   gate              — deterministic compute: converged = tree green
##                       AND scope clean AND campaign reports docs
##                       aligned AND manifest coverage ≥ target. The
##                       loop re-runs build_manifest each pass so the
##                       drift set always reflects the live tree.
##   mark_issue_for_review / update_audit_cache — dispatcher issue
##                       transition + inter-run cache write (unchanged;
##                       the cache now records the manifest's mechanical
##                       verified_pairs instead of reviewer claims).
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 loop was ~16 nodes: the audit machinery above
## PLUS an alternating cross-family review/fix assembly line
## (alt condition router → reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt → streak_check
## → fix_claude / fix_gpt → prepare_commit → commit_changes →
## detect_doc_changes) with streak/coverage/chronic-path/dismissed-pairs
## accumulators to keep it from oscillating. ADR-058
## (docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is
## framing, not capability" — a capable agent adjudicates a bounded,
## pre-verified drift manifest and fixes the real drifts better as ONE
## flow than as a reviewer/fixer relay, and the accumulator machinery
## existed only to police the relay's oscillation. The deterministic
## manifest + scope + build gates keep every anti-Goodhart guarantee.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (build_manifest→campaign→scope_check→verify_build→verify_run→gate→
## build_manifest) is capped by `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships
## what is banked (every committed doc alignment is real, landed work).
## `iterion validate` reports NO undeclared cycle (author_rescan(1) is
## the one other declared loop, unchanged from v1).
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic"):
## universal doc_globs defaults, empty code scope = whole workspace,
## the CLI/diagnostic surface scan stays opt-in (Cobra-shaped repos),
## and the cache is a repo-root dotfile. Unchanged from v1.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): the `alt` condition
## router, reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt, streak_check (+ its
## cumulative_dismissed_pairs / cumulative_pushback / chronic-path
## accumulators — nothing left to oscillate), fix_claude / fix_gpt,
## prepare_commit / commit_changes (the campaign commits in stride),
## detect_doc_changes (both remaining terminal tools are ~ms and
## unconditional), enforce_fix_scope (reborn as the detect-only
## scope_check), and the review_mode / mono_family topology vars —
## the ADR-052 mono/dual review topology no longer applies to this bot
## (no reviewer nodes). 16 nodes → 11.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Doc footprint scope (comma-separated globs). The scan_docs
  ## tool node enumerates files matching these globs once, at run
  ## start. Hidden / vendored / runtime dirs (.iterion, .works,
  ## .claude, vendor, node_modules, .git, dist, build, out) are
  ## excluded unconditionally inside the scanner; the bot's own
  ## bundle is excluded when `bundle_self_path` is set.
  ## UNIVERSAL DEFAULT: docs-refresh documents ANY repo, so the default
  ## footprint is the conventions common to most projects (root +
  ## nested READMEs, a docs/ tree, CLAUDE.md). Repo-specific doc
  ## locations are added per-repo via --var, never baked into the
  ## catalog default. See "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" in CLAUDE.md.
  doc_globs: string = "README.md,docs/**/*.md,**/README.md,CLAUDE.md,**/CLAUDE.md"

  ## Go code comment scope (comma-separated globs). Empty (default)
  ## means: do NOT audit Go function/package comments — speeds up
  ## first runs. Non-empty also widens the campaign's writeable set
  ## (and scope_check's allow-list) to comment-only edits in those
  ## files.
  go_comment_globs: string = ""

  ## Code scope the campaign may grep / read to verify doc claims.
  ## UNIVERSAL DEFAULT: empty = the campaign may grep the whole
  ## workspace (minus `excluded_dirs`).
  code_scope_globs: string = ""

  ## Optional free-text notes pinning the campaign's attention.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## Coverage threshold (percentage of mechanically-checkable anchors
  ## the manifest must have VERIFIED before the gate can converge).
  ## Default 80 — high enough to defeat "declare aligned after a
  ## partial pass", low enough to tolerate a genuinely-unverifiable
  ## tail. The gate compares build_manifest.coverage_pct against it
  ## every pass; the campaign raises it by fixing drifted anchors.
  coverage_target_pct: int = 80

  ## Auto-exclusion. When non-empty, scan_docs excludes any file whose
  ## path starts with this prefix from the doc footprint. Set to the
  ## bot's own bundle dir when running docs-refresh on a repo that
  ## contains its source; otherwise leave empty.
  bundle_self_path: string = ""

  ## Incremental mode. When non-empty, scan_docs computes
  ## `git diff <diff_since>...HEAD --name-only` and surfaces the result
  ## as `recently_changed_code_files` — a prioritisation HINT for the
  ## campaign, never a scope reduction. Useful for nightly / per-PR
  ## runs (`--var diff_since=main`).
  diff_since: string = ""

  ## Hard-excluded directories. Comma-separated list of directory names
  ## whose presence anywhere in a path disqualifies it from `doc_files`
  ## (regardless of `doc_globs`). Defaults exclude runtime / scratch /
  ## vendor / artifact dirs that should never be audited. Setting to
  ## empty disables all exclusion (NOT recommended — picks up `.git/`).
  excluded_dirs: string = ".iterion,.works,.claude,vendor,node_modules,.git,dist,build,out"

  ## Code-surface scan scope. Globs for the `scan_code_surface` tool —
  ## a counter-omission audit that greps the code for publicly-exposed
  ## identifiers docs ought to mention (CLI commands, flags, diagnostic
  ## codes) and checks each appears in at least one doc.
  ## OPT-IN / NON-UNIVERSAL: the extraction patterns are specialised
  ## for a Cobra-CLI + diagnostic-code repo shape; the catalog DEFAULT
  ## is empty = surface scan DISABLED. A CLI/DSL repo opts in per-run,
  ## e.g. --var cli_surface_globs="cmd/myapp/*.go".
  cli_surface_globs: string = ""
  diagnostic_surface_globs: string = ""

  ## Cap on the campaign's per-pass working set — build_manifest sorts
  ## candidates by severity (drifted CLI surface > diagnostic >
  ## file_ref > symbol_ref > unverifiable) then takes the top N. The
  ## v0.15 lesson: an unbounded candidate list blows the context
  ## window; the campaign ITERATES — pass 1 fixes ~a chunk, pass 2
  ## sees the next tier (or coverage rises).
  max_drift_candidates: int = 40

  ## Cap on the number of DISTINCT docs whose candidates may land in a
  ## single pass (applied after the severity sort, before the
  ## candidate cap). Bounds the file surface the campaign is tempted
  ## to spot-check per pass (the v0.16 context lesson). Convergence is
  ## unchanged: the gate uses the anchor-level coverage_pct across ALL
  ## docs. 0 disables doc-chunking.
  max_review_chunk_docs: int = 30

  ## Identifier of the tracker issue this run was dispatched from.
  ## Used by mark_issue_for_review at the end of the workflow to
  ## transition the issue out of `ready` so the dispatcher doesn't
  ## re-pick it on the next poll. Filled by the dispatcher via
  ## `vars.issue_id: "{{issue.identifier}}"`. Empty when run outside
  ## the dispatcher — mark_issue_for_review is then a no-op.
  issue_id: string = ""

  ## Inter-run audit cache. Path to the JSON cache file that stores
  ## `{git_head, doc: {doc_path: {sha, refs: [{p, s}]}}}` from the
  ## previous successful run. scan_docs reads this and emits
  ## `pre_verified_docs` for entries whose doc content AND all cited
  ## code-file SHAs are unchanged, and short-circuits the whole run
  ## (noop_skip) when git_head still matches the live HEAD on a clean
  ## tree; update_audit_cache (the terminal tool) writes the merged
  ## state from the manifest's mechanically VERIFIED pairs.
  ## Empty string disables both reads and writes.
  ## UNIVERSAL DEFAULT: a persistent, OUT-OF-TREE file under the
  ## project scratch dir (keyed off the original repo root, so it
  ## survives across runs). A repo-root dotfile would NOT survive
  ## under `worktree: auto` — the run executes in a fresh worktree
  ## that omits untracked files, so a repo-relative cache is written
  ## into the ephemeral worktree and lost on finalize (which also
  ## silently defeats pre_verified + the noop gate). Scratch is
  ## host-persistent when not sandboxed (the nightly/scheduled case);
  ## under sandbox it is per-run, so the cache simply no-ops (safe
  ## degradation — noop never wrongly skips). Set to a repo-relative
  ## path for a non-worktree run, or empty to disable.
  audit_cache_path: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/docs-refresh-cache.json"

  ## DEFAULT-CREATE: when scan_docs finds ZERO documentation files in
  ## scope, the `author_docs` node creates this directory and authors
  ## an initial documentation set there from the code; the run then
  ## re-scans (author_rescan) and the normal campaign polishes +
  ## commits it. Repo-relative; DEFAULT "docs" (covered by the default
  ## doc_globs). If you point it OUTSIDE the default doc_globs, widen
  ## doc_globs too so the post-authoring re-scan picks the new docs up.
  docs_dir: string = "docs"

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY. The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}
  ## to ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox. Keeping it
  ## OUT of the target worktree is the repo-agnostic rule.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/docs-refresh"

  ## BASELINE (G5). Known pre-existing build/test failures the campaign
  ## must SKIP rather than burn budget proving they aren't its own —
  ## docs-refresh rarely trips this (docs-only edits), but when
  ## go_comment_globs opts comment edits in, the build gate runs and a
  ## red base tree must not absorb the run. Empty = establish once
  ## cheaply, then skip what predates the run.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each
  ## pass is one manifest→campaign→verify cycle; the run stops
  ## (shipping what is banked) when the docs are reported aligned +
  ## the gates are green, or when this cap is hit.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── PR finalization (opt-in) ─────────────────────────────────
  ## Same contract as feature-dev/app-dev: when open_mr=true, after the
  ## run finishes (converged OR max_passes exhausted with banked
  ## commits), finalize_mr pushes the doc-alignment series and opens
  ## the pull request (PR; merge request on GitLab). Requires a git
  ## remote + a push credential (forge_token secret, *_TOKEN env, or
  ## host gh auth) — the deterministic forge_auth_probe skips the tail
  ## cleanly when none exists, so local runs keep today's behavior
  ## (commits stay on the run's storage branch). The noop_skip path
  ## never reaches this tail, and finalize_mr refuses to open an empty
  ## PR when no commits landed.
  open_mr: bool = false
  mr_branch: string = ""
  mr_base: string = ""
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + PR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted
  ## as a read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read
  ## into a prompt. optional:true so local runs (no forge token bound)
  ## and host-authenticated gh/glab setups work unchanged.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior technical writer-engineer running a DOC-ALIGNMENT
  campaign — working EXACTLY as you would in a productive, human-driven
  session. You have the repo in front of you and full tools (read, edit,
  shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass end to end.

  THE MISSION: make the documentation match the actual current state of
  the code. DOCS FOLLOW CODE; code does NOT follow docs — you correct
  documentation, never the other way around.

  LOAD YOUR SKILLS FIRST. The bundle ships five skills under
  `.claude/skills/` describing the discipline you MUST follow:
    - `docs-refresh.md` — operating playbook, the why and the
      inviolable rules
    - `doc-mismatch-taxonomy.md` — the mismatch kinds and anchor kinds
      that tell you what a real drift looks like
    - `doc-scope-enumeration.md` — the immutable scan_docs footprint
      contract
    - `doc-verification-checklist.md` — the verification preamble to
      run before declaring anything aligned
    - `anti-facade-fix-rules.md` — what a façade fix looks like

  YOUR WORKING SET is deterministic. Upstream, `build_manifest` has
  already enumerated every code anchor in every doc in scope and
  verified each against the live tree. Your user prompt carries the
  bounded `drift_candidates` slice (severity-sorted, doc-chunked):
    - status `drifted`      — the anchor is NOT in the live code (file
      missing, flag absent, code renamed). Highest signal: verify, then
      FIX the doc.
    - status `unverifiable` — the heuristic couldn't resolve it (prose
      that looks like CamelCase, archived refs, external links). Judge
      from the doc context; most are false positives to LEAVE ALONE.
  The mechanical coverage_pct in your prompt is the gate's coverage
  oracle — it rises as you fix drifted anchors; docs the manifest
  chunk deferred will surface on a later pass.

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Read the manifest summary +
     candidates and build a todo grouped BY DOC (all of one doc's
     drifts together beat scattered single-anchor edits). Use
     `recently_changed_code_files` (when present) to prioritise; keep
     the list DENSE and MOVING as you learn.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one doc at a time: for each candidate in that
     doc, READ the code at the cited anchor to verify the current
     state (the tool invocation — read_file / grep — is your evidence;
     a fix whose rationale cannot cite it is a façade) → apply the
     smallest doc correction that makes the claim true → run the
     negative-space check (`git grep` for stale cross-references to
     whatever you renamed/removed, fix those too) → COMMIT the aligned
     doc with a semantic message (`docs(<area>): <what was aligned>`),
     body ending with the trailer line `Bot: docs-refresh`. Use
     `fix(<area>):` ONLY when you fixed a demonstrably misleading code
     COMMENT (go_comment_globs opt-in).

  3. COMMIT EACH DOC AS YOU ALIGN IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`) and commit before moving on.
     git IS your durable state: an interrupted run keeps every doc you
     committed, and a fresh pass just reads `git log` + the fresh
     manifest and continues. Do NOT keep a separate worklist file.

  4. WRITEABLE SET — `.md` files in the doc footprint, plus (ONLY when
     `go_comment_globs` is non-empty) comment-only edits in Go files
     matching those globs. NEVER touch code statements, configs, or
     build files. A deterministic scope gate diffs the whole run
     against its base after every pass: any out-of-scope file FAILS
     the gate and bounces back to you — you would then have to revert
     those commits (`git revert` or checkout from the run base) before
     continuing. Don't make it fire.

  5. ESCAPE VALVE — is_code_bug. When a doc is CORRECT and the CODE is
     wrong, do NOT "align" the doc to broken behaviour. Set
     is_code_bug=true in your termination output, name the case in
     human_note, and (for a genuinely blocking ambiguity) pause via
     the ask-user tool. Filing it as a board finding (see below) is
     the default; pausing is for when you cannot proceed without the
     answer.

  6. BASELINE — don't chase failures you didn't cause. A build/test
     failure that predates this run (see `baseline`, or establish it
     once cheaply with a `git stash`-based check) is out of scope:
     note it and move on. Docs-only passes rarely trip the build gate;
     comment edits can.

  7. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. Keep operator pauses RARE — most
     drift adjudications are yours to make; lower-stakes doubts go in
     `summary`.

  FINDINGS HANDOFF — after your last commit, capture anything OUT OF
  SCOPE but worth tracking (a code bug found while verifying an
  anchor, an undocumented capability worth its own ticket, an
  infrastructure issue): call `mcp__iterion_board__list_issues` with
  `{ "state": "inbox" }` first to avoid duplicates, then
  `mcp__iterion_board__create_issue` per NEW finding with state
  "inbox" and labels ["findings", "kind:<bug|drift|improvement|
  question>", "source:docs_refresh", "area:<subsystem>",
  "severity:<low|medium|high>"]. Zero findings is a fine outcome. If
  the board tools are unavailable, list the findings in `summary`.

  KEEP GOING, one doc at a time, until this pass has adjudicated every
  candidate in its working set. Then STOP and report.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the
  engine detects completion; an under-specified end just gets
  re-poked):
    - docs_aligned: true ONLY when every candidate this pass surfaced
      is either fixed (committed) or adjudicated a false positive —
      i.e. you expect a fresh manifest re-scan to find no remaining
      REAL drift. false if real drift remains (a later pass continues
      from your commits, with a fresh manifest).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - drift_remaining: a concise note of what still drifts (empty when
      docs_aligned).
    - is_code_bug: true when you found ≥1 place where the doc is right
      and the code is wrong (detail in human_note / the board finding).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator
      decision.
    - summary: which docs you aligned (with the verification evidence
      pattern you used), which candidates you dismissed as false
      positives and why.

  Be honest. A deterministic manifest re-scan, a scope gate and a
  build gate re-check the tree after you, and the run loops you back
  until you report docs_aligned AND the gates are green AND the
  mechanical coverage meets its target — so under-reporting only costs
  a pass, and over-reporting lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Scope notes (operator's attention pin, may be empty):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  ## Deterministic manifest summary (this pass)
  Total docs scanned:       {{input.total_docs}}
  Total anchors enumerated: {{input.total_anchors}}
  Verified:                 {{input.verified_anchors}}
  Drifted:                  {{input.drifted_anchors}}
  Unverifiable:             {{input.unverifiable_anchors}}
  Mechanical coverage:      {{input.manifest_coverage_pct}}% (gate target: {{input.coverage_target_pct}}%)

  ## This pass's chunk
  Docs with remaining drift: {{input.docs_with_drift_count}}
  Docs in this chunk:        {{input.chunk_doc_count}} (cap: {{input.max_review_chunk_docs}})
  Chunked this pass:         {{input.chunked}}
  When chunked=true, drifts in the deferred docs surface on a later
  pass as you clear this chunk — do NOT report docs_aligned=true
  while docs_with_drift_count exceeds what this chunk shows fixed.

  ## Drift candidates (the working set — severity-sorted, bounded)
  Each entry: {doc, line, kind, value, status, evidence, excerpt}.
  {{input.drift_candidates}}

  Recently changed code files (prioritisation hint, may be empty):
  {{input.recently_changed_code_files}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures to SKIP (empty = establish once
  cheaply if the build gate goes red):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the
  first pass; when present it is EITHER a scope violation — files
  outside the doc writeable-set changed; revert those commits before
  continuing — OR a real build/test failure to fix):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  Work the candidates one doc at a time — verify at the anchor →
  smallest doc fix → negative-space check → commit (`docs(<area>): …`
  + `Bot: docs-refresh` trailer) — then report the termination
  contract (docs_aligned, commits_this_pass, drift_remaining).
  Read `git log` first to see what earlier passes already aligned.

## ─── Prompts : Default-create (author docs from zero) ──────────────

prompt author_docs_system:
  You are the doc author for the `docs-refresh` workflow, invoked on a
  repository that currently has NO documentation in scope. Your job is
  to BOOTSTRAP an initial documentation set by reading the code and
  writing accurate Markdown, so the run's normal review/refresh loop has
  something real to polish.

  Load the `docs-refresh.md` skill for the operating context.

  WHAT TO CREATE:
   - Create the docs directory (`{{input.docs_dir}}`) if it does not
     exist, and author your documentation there as `.md` files.
   - Author or refresh a top-level `README.md` if the repo has none — a
     short overview that links into `{{input.docs_dir}}/`.
   - Cover, at minimum: a project overview (what it is / what it does),
     how to build & run it, the high-level architecture or module map,
     and how to use the main entry points. Add further pages only where
     the code clearly warrants them — do not pad.

  HARD CONSTRAINTS:
   1. Documentation only — write `.md` files. NEVER modify source code,
      build files, or configuration.
   2. EVIDENCE OVER INVENTION. Every statement must reflect what the
      code actually does. Read the relevant files before describing
      them; never document aspirational or assumed behaviour. If you
      cannot verify a claim, omit it rather than guess.
   3. STACK-AGNOSTIC. This repo may be in ANY language. Detect the
      language / build system from the repo itself (manifest files,
      entry points, lockfiles) — never assume a particular ecosystem.
   4. Do NOT commit. A later step commits on convergence.

prompt author_docs_user:
  Author initial documentation for the repository at
  {{input.workspace_dir}}.

  Target docs directory (create if absent): {{input.docs_dir}}

  Recently changed code files (a hint, may be empty — non-authoritative):
  {{input.recently_changed_code_files}}

  Steps:
   1. Survey the repository to understand its purpose, language/stack,
      structure, and entry points (read manifest files, top-level
      directories, and the main entry file(s)).
   2. Create `{{input.docs_dir}}/` if needed and author the initial
      `.md` pages described in your system prompt, each grounded in what
      you read. Author/refresh a root `README.md` overview if missing.
   3. Keep each page focused and accurate; cross-link the pages.

  Output fields:
   - docs_created: true if you created at least one doc file
   - created_doc_files: repo-relative paths you wrote
   - summary: what you authored, and the evidence (files you read)
     behind the key claims

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a
## DETERMINISTIC tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit
## code. Docs-only passes should always be green here; the gate earns its
## keep when go_comment_globs opts comment edits in (the tree must still
## compile) and as the universal engine-side floor shared with the other v2
## bots. verify_build does NOT fix anything: a RED gate routes back to
## `campaign`.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A doc-alignment pass just committed changes
  to this repository (documentation, possibly code COMMENTS). Your single
  job: capture how THIS repo builds and tests itself into a re-runnable
  script the deterministic gate will execute. You do NOT change anything.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain, HONOURING any pinned toolchain
     (version managers, lockfiles, devbox, GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a
     language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build (and the
     fast test targets a comment-only change could plausibly affect) and
     exits non-zero on any failure. For a docs-only repo or a pass that
     touched only `.md` files, a build-only script (or the closest cheap
     sanity command) is appropriate — keep it FAST.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: scope `verify.sh` so a red base tree can't
     absorb the run; list any excluded pre-existing failures in your
     summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid. You do NOT fix
     anything here.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config),
     write a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its
  real exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  exists; summary = the command you settled on.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build (+ fast tests where comment edits could
  plausibly matter) into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` (out-of-tree —
  see the system prompt + the `verify-build` skill). Do NOT change
  anything; the deterministic gate runs your script and the campaign
  fixes any red on the next pass.

## ─── PR finalization prompts (opt-in) ──────────────────────────────

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a finished docs-refresh run by opening ONE pull request
  (PR; merge request on GitLab) for the SERIES of per-doc alignment
  commits this run produced, then (optionally) posting the PR link back
  to the source issue. You make NO further changes to docs or code.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and
  open the PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of
       the base (`git rev-list --count <base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do
       NOT open an empty PR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/docs-refresh/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set,
       else the repo's default branch — see the skill).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token
       file when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is
       available, set opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never
       pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the PR
       from that branch into the base. Title: a concise
       "docs: …" conventional summary. Body: the campaign summary, the
       docs aligned this run, and — when drift_remaining is non-empty —
       an honest "Remaining drift" section (the run shipped what is
       banked; never present a partial alignment as complete).
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened PR URL back to it: a
       forge issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>"
       ref → use the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short
  summary. Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → repo default branch): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Operator scope notes (may be empty): {{input.scope_notes}}

  Campaign summary (for the PR body):
  {{input.campaign_summary}}

  Remaining drift reported by the campaign (empty = fully aligned):
  {{input.drift_remaining}}

  Push the branch, open the PR, post the back-link when a source issue
  is given, and return the structured result.

## ─── Schemas (v2 campaign / gates) ─────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the bounded manifest working set for this pass +
## the deterministic gate feedback from the previous pass (empty on the
## first pass and on a green-but-more-drift continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  total_docs: int
  total_anchors: int
  verified_anchors: int
  drifted_anchors: int
  unverifiable_anchors: int
  manifest_coverage_pct: int
  coverage_target_pct: int
  drift_candidates: json
  docs_with_drift_count: int
  chunked: bool
  chunk_doc_count: int
  max_review_chunk_docs: int
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]
  fail_log: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the
## engine keys convergence on. docs_aligned is the done-oracle (this
## pass's candidates are all fixed-or-dismissed and no real drift is
## expected on a fresh re-scan); the gate ANDs it with the mechanical
## coverage, the scope check and the build gate.
schema campaign_output:
  docs_aligned: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  drift_remaining: string
  is_code_bug: bool
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC build gate's contract. `passed` is
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT
## an LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was
## produced (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## scope_check is the DETERMINISTIC writeable-set gate (v2 rewrite of
## v1's enforce_fix_scope): every path changed since the RUN BASE must
## be a doc (`.md`), the audit cache file, or (when go_comment_globs is
## non-empty) a file inside those globs. Out-of-scope changes fail the
## gate and bounce back to the campaign, which must revert them — with
## git-as-state the tree is committed, so detection+bounce replaces
## v1's silent working-tree checkout-revert.
schema scope_check_output:
  scope_ok: bool
  out_of_scope: string[]
  log: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = build
## green AND scope clean AND the campaign reports the docs aligned AND
## the manifest's mechanical coverage meets the target. fail_log relays
## the scope violation (priority) or the real build failure to the
## campaign on a red pass; empty when green.
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

## ─── PR finalization schemas (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output field so the two
## outgoing edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool

## forge_auth_probe output — deterministic "can we push?" so finalize_mr (an
## LLM agent) is only ever entered when a push credential actually exists.
schema forge_auth_state:
  available: bool
  reason: string

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string
  campaign_summary: string
  drift_remaining: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string


## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## code_surface (v0.6.0): the inventory of "publicly-exposed"
## identifiers extracted from the workspace by the
## `scan_code_surface` tool. build_manifest compares these with the
## documentation as a counter-omission audit; the campaign verifies
## and documents any real `undocumented_capability` drift.
schema code_surface:
  cli_commands: string[]
  cli_flags: string[]
  diagnostic_codes: string[]
  scan_skipped: string

## scan_output is the immutable doc footprint emitted by the
## upstream scan_docs tool node. recently_changed_code_files is
## populated from `git diff` when vars.diff_since is set
## (otherwise empty array). bundle_self_path is echoed for
## transparency.
##
## v0.5.0 adds `pre_verified_docs`: docs whose content AND all
## referenced code-file SHAs match what was recorded at the configured
## audit_cache_path after the previous successful run. build_manifest
## treats their mechanically verified pairs as already checked and
## focuses the current pass on changed regions.
schema scan_output:
  doc_files: string[]
  doc_count: int
  ## DEFAULT-CREATE gate (v0.3.0): true when doc_count == 0 (no docs in
  ## scope). Routes scan_docs -> author_docs (bootstrap an initial doc
  ## set) instead of straight into the campaign. After authoring, the
  ## run re-scans; no_docs is then false and the normal path runs.
  no_docs: bool
  footprint_hash: string
  scope_globs: string[]
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]
  bundle_self_path: string
  pre_verified_docs: string[]
  cache_hits: int
  cache_misses: int
  ## noop gate (openwiki-inspired): true when the docs are already aligned
  ## to the exact current HEAD (cache git_head matches, clean worktree) and
  ## no explicit request (issue_id) is present — routes scan_docs -> done,
  ## skipping the whole campaign. noop_reason explains the decision either
  ## way; git_head is the current HEAD (empty outside a git repo).
  noop_skip: bool
  noop_reason: string
  git_head: string

schema author_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  docs_dir: string
  recently_changed_code_files: string[]

schema author_output:
  docs_created: bool
  created_doc_files: string[]
  summary: string

## v0.15.4: schema for mark_issue_for_review's free-form output.
## The tool returns JSON-as-string on success ({"id":...,"state":...})
## or {"skipped":"no issue_id"} on the manual-CLI no-op path. We
## don't consume the value downstream — the schema exists to make
## the tool node DSL-valid.
schema mark_issue_output:
  text: string

## update_audit_cache I/O (v0.5.0). The `audited_pairs: string[]`
## field is shell-tokenised by iterion's template substitution
## (`'pair1' 'pair2' 'pair3'`) so each element lands as a separate
## argv entry to python3 — env var passing breaks on internal
## whitespace, JSON encoding is unreachable via the {{!...}} raw
## form because raw values still go through env-var assignment.
schema update_audit_cache_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  cache_path: string
  audited_pairs: string[]

schema update_audit_cache_output:
  cache_path: string
  entries_written: int
  ## HEAD committed into the cache; the NEXT run's scan_docs compares it
  ## against the live HEAD for the noop short-circuit.
  git_head: string
  skipped: string


## build_manifest I/O (v0.15.0 manifest, consumed by the v2 campaign).
##
## Replaces ad-hoc agent enumeration with a
## deterministic anchor extraction + verification pass. For each
## doc in the footprint, scans for code references (file paths,
## symbols, CLI commands, CLI flags, diagnostic codes, markdown
## link targets), then checks each anchor against the live code
## state (scan_code_surface outputs + filesystem). The campaign
## adjudicates the bounded drift candidates with pre-fetched evidence,
## then reads only the live anchors needed for semantic verification.
##
## The before/after on the 2026-05-21 v1 dogfood: reviewer_gpt's iter
## 1 burned through 4 LLM steps and ~400K input tokens enumerating
## anchors via grep before hitting context_length_exceeded. Under
## the manifest pattern, the same iter receives ~150 pre-resolved
## candidates with file:line evidence — bounded context, ~5 tool
## steps to spot-check the high-severity ones, structured verdict.
schema build_manifest_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  doc_files: string[]
  cli_commands: string[]
  cli_flags: string[]
  diagnostic_codes: string[]
  code_scope_globs: string
  ## max_drift_candidates caps the drift_candidates[] slice so the
  ## campaign prompt stays bounded regardless of how degraded the
  ## docs are. Full counts are still reported for the deterministic gate.
  max_drift_candidates: int
  ## v0.16.0: max distinct docs whose candidates may appear in a
  ## single chunk. Applied after the severity sort, before the
  ## max_drift_candidates cap. 0 disables doc-chunking.
  max_review_chunk_docs: int

schema build_manifest_output:
  ## total_docs / total_anchors are the cardinality of the
  ## deterministic enumeration. Per-doc breakdown lives in
  ## per_doc_anchor_counts (bounded by doc count, not anchor count).
  total_docs: int
  total_anchors: int
  ## verified_anchors counts anchors whose existence/presence the
  ## tool confirmed mechanically (file exists, CLI command in
  ## scan_code_surface output, diagnostic code present, etc.).
  ## drifted_anchors = total - verified - unverifiable.
  verified_anchors: int
  drifted_anchors: int
  unverifiable_anchors: int
  ## checkable_anchors = verified + drifted (excludes unverifiable).
  ## coverage_pct = verified / checkable, so anchors whose scanner was
  ## skipped/unscoped don't drag the gate below reach.
  checkable_anchors: int
  coverage_pct: int
  ## drift_candidates is the campaign's working set. Each entry
  ## carries the doc::anchor pair plus pre-fetched evidence so the
  ## campaign can judge "real drift vs false positive" without
  ## re-enumerating the tree. Capped at input.max_drift_candidates;
  ## when more candidates exist, the entries are prioritised by
  ## severity (removed/drifted CLI surface > drifted file_ref >
  ## unverifiable symbol).
  drift_candidates: json
  ## per_doc_anchor_counts is the deterministic coverage signal —
  ## each entry says "doc X had Y anchors total, Z of them verified".
  ## The gate uses coverage_pct; this list exists for telemetry/reports.
  per_doc_anchor_counts: json
  ## all_audited_docs lists every doc the manifest scanned. Retained
  ## as telemetry/backward-compatible output; the manifest pass itself
  ## is the audit and the v2 gate reads coverage_pct directly.
  all_audited_docs: string[]
  ## v0.16.0 doc-count chunking telemetry. `docs_with_drift_count`
  ## is the universe of docs that still have at least one drift
  ## candidate this pass (before chunking). `chunk_doc_count` is
  ## the count of distinct docs whose candidates landed in this
  ## pass's slice. `chunked=true` when chunk_doc_count <
  ## docs_with_drift_count (some docs deferred to a later pass).
  ## `max_review_chunk_docs` echoes the compatibility-named var so the
  ## campaign prompt can show the active cap.
  docs_with_drift_count: int
  chunked: bool
  chunk_doc_count: int
  max_review_chunk_docs: int
  ## v2: the mechanically VERIFIED doc::anchor pairs — feeds the
  ## inter-run audit cache (update_audit_cache) so pre-verification no
  ## longer depends on retired reviewer claims.
  verified_pairs: string[]

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## scan_docs runs once upstream of the campaign (and once more only on
## the bounded zero-doc author_rescan path). Emits the
## immutable doc footprint + (when vars.diff_since is set) the
## list of code files changed since that ref. Auto-excludes the
## bot's own bundle via vars.bundle_self_path (fix 4).
##
## Raw-string command — the python snippet embeds quotes and the
## raw-string form avoids escape hell. Templates {{vars.X}}
## resolve at iterion's command-substitution layer; the values
## are then exposed to python via shell env vars (WS, GLOBS,
## BUNDLE_SELF, DIFF_SINCE).
tool scan_docs:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} GLOBS={{vars.doc_globs}} BUNDLE_SELF={{vars.bundle_self_path}} DIFF_SINCE={{vars.diff_since}} CACHE_PATH={{vars.audit_cache_path}} EXCLUDED_DIRS={{vars.excluded_dirs}} ISSUE_ID={{vars.issue_id}} python3 -c "
import os, json, glob, hashlib, subprocess
ws = os.environ['WS']
globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ['GLOBS'].split(',') if g.strip()]
bundle_self = os.environ.get('BUNDLE_SELF', '').strip().rstrip(os.sep)
diff_since = os.environ.get('DIFF_SINCE', '').strip()
cache_path = os.environ.get('CACHE_PATH', '').strip()
os.chdir(ws)
EXCLUDE_DIRS = {d.strip() for d in os.environ.get('EXCLUDED_DIRS', '').split(',') if d.strip()}
def excluded(p):
    if any(part in EXCLUDE_DIRS for part in p.split(os.sep)):
        return True
    if bundle_self and (p == bundle_self or p.startswith(bundle_self + os.sep)):
        return True
    return False
def sha_of_file(p):
    try:
        with open(p, 'rb') as fh:
            return hashlib.sha1(fh.read()).hexdigest()
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        return 'missing'
files = sorted({f for g in globs for f in glob.glob(g, recursive=True) if os.path.isfile(f) and not excluded(f)})
h = hashlib.sha1(chr(10).join(files).encode()).hexdigest()
recent = []
if diff_since:
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['git', 'diff', '--name-only', diff_since + '...HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
        recent = sorted([l for l in proc.stdout.splitlines() if l and not excluded(l)])
    except Exception:
        recent = []
pre_verified = []
hits = 0
misses = 0
cache_head = ''
if cache_path:
    try:
        with open(cache_path) as cf:
            cache = json.load(cf)
        cache_head = cache.get('git_head', '') or ''
        doc_entries = cache.get('doc', {})
        for doc_path in files:
            entry = doc_entries.get(doc_path)
            if not entry:
                misses += 1
                continue
            current_sha = sha_of_file(doc_path)
            if entry.get('sha') != current_sha:
                misses += 1
                continue
            refs_ok = True
            for ref in entry.get('refs', []):
                p = ref.get('p', '')
                s = ref.get('s', '')
                if not p:
                    continue
                current = sha_of_file(p) if os.path.exists(p) else 'missing'
                if current != s:
                    refs_ok = False
                    break
            if refs_ok:
                pre_verified.append(doc_path)
                hits += 1
            else:
                misses += 1
    except (FileNotFoundError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        pass
# noop detection (openwiki-inspired whole-run skip): when the docs are
# already aligned to the exact current HEAD and no human explicitly asked,
# skip the entire (expensive) campaign. update_audit_cache persists git_head
# in the cache on every successful terminal run; a matching HEAD + clean
# worktree proves nothing has changed since that alignment. issue_id (a
# dispatched ticket) or scope_notes is an explicit request and never skips —
# the parity of openwiki's 'disable noop when a user message is supplied'.
issue_id = os.environ.get('ISSUE_ID', '').strip()
cache_abs = os.path.abspath(cache_path) if cache_path else ''
def git_head():
    try:
        return subprocess.run(['git', 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        return ''
def worktree_dirty():
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['git', 'status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=all'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
    except Exception:
        return True
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        path = line[3:].strip() if len(line) > 3 else ''
        if not path:
            continue
        # ignore the audit cache itself (it may be an untracked in-tree
        # override; the out-of-tree default never appears here anyway)
        if cache_abs and os.path.abspath(path) == cache_abs:
            continue
        if excluded(path):
            continue
        return True
    return False
head = git_head()
noop_skip = False
if len(files) == 0:
    noop_reason = 'no docs in scope'
elif issue_id:
    noop_reason = 'explicit request (issue_id set)'
elif not head:
    noop_reason = 'HEAD unavailable (not a git repo?)'
elif not cache_head:
    noop_reason = 'no cached git_head (first aligned run)'
elif head != cache_head:
    noop_reason = 'HEAD advanced since last alignment'
elif worktree_dirty():
    noop_reason = 'worktree has uncommitted changes'
else:
    noop_skip = True
    noop_reason = 'docs already aligned to HEAD ' + head[:12]
print(json.dumps({'doc_files': files, 'doc_count': len(files), 'no_docs': len(files) == 0, 'footprint_hash': h, 'scope_globs': globs, 'recently_changed_code_files': recent, 'bundle_self_path': bundle_self, 'pre_verified_docs': pre_verified, 'cache_hits': hits, 'cache_misses': misses, 'noop_skip': noop_skip, 'noop_reason': noop_reason, 'git_head': head}))
"`
  output: scan_output

## scan_code_surface (v0.6.0): runs after scan_docs and extracts
## "publicly-exposed identifiers" from the workspace via grep so the
## manifest/campaign can audit code→doc presence (the inverse direction
## of the existing doc→code audit). Categories:
##   - cli_commands: cobra `Use:` literals in cmd/iterion/*.go
##   - cli_flags: --flag-name tokens in StringVar/BoolVar/...Var
##     calls inside the same files
##   - diagnostic_codes: "Cxxx" constants in pkg/dsl/ir/*.go and
##     pkg/dsl/parser/*.go
##
## When `cli_surface_globs` AND `diagnostic_surface_globs` are
## both empty, the scan is skipped and the output is empty lists
## with scan_skipped set to a reason string. Useful for runs
## targeting docs that have no CLI / diagnostic surface (e.g.
## a library-only repo).
##
## OPT-IN, iterion-shaped SPECIALIZATION — not a universal default.
## The `cobra Use:` / `--flag` / `Cxxx` patterns below only yield
## meaningful output on a repo with iterion's own code shape, so this
## scanner ships OFF (both surface globs empty by default). A run
## against any other repo either leaves it skipped or points the globs
## at that repo's own surface patterns via --var. Generalising these
## regexes to another stack is the bar for making it a default. See
## CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic".
tool scan_code_surface:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} CLI_GLOBS={{vars.cli_surface_globs}} DIAG_GLOBS={{vars.diagnostic_surface_globs}} python3 -c "
import os, json, glob, re
ws = os.environ['WS']
cli_globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('CLI_GLOBS','').split(',') if g.strip()]
diag_globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('DIAG_GLOBS','').split(',') if g.strip()]
os.chdir(ws)
if not cli_globs and not diag_globs:
    print(json.dumps({'cli_commands': [], 'cli_flags': [], 'diagnostic_codes': [], 'scan_skipped': 'both globs empty'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
cli_files = sorted({f for g in cli_globs for f in glob.glob(g, recursive=True) if os.path.isfile(f)})
diag_files = sorted({f for g in diag_globs for f in glob.glob(g, recursive=True) if os.path.isfile(f)})
## v0.15.3: Use: pattern allows optional [args] / <args> suffixes inside
## the quoted string (cobra convention for declaring positional args).
## Previously the regex required \" immediately after the captured name,
## so 'Use: \"dispatch [config.yaml]\"' didn't match and 'dispatch' was
## reported as a drifted CLI command on every iter.
cmd_re = re.compile(r'Use:\s*\"([a-zA-Z0-9_\-]+)(?:[\s<\[\{].*?)?\"')
## v0.15.3: two flag patterns:
##   - Var family: BoolVar(&ptr, \"name\", ...) — takes a pointer first
##   - Non-Var: cobra's Flags().String(\"name\", ...) without backing var
## Both with optional P suffix (StringP, BoolP) for shorthand variants.
## Previously the Non-Var family was unscanned, so 'paths' and 'format'
## (declared via Flags().StringSlice / Flags().String in cmd/iterion/
## bots.go) were reported as drifted on every iter. The negative
## lookahead (?!Get) excludes the read variants Flags().GetString etc.
flag_var_re = re.compile(r'(?:String|Bool|Int|Duration|Float64|Int64|StringSlice|StringArray)Var\(\s*&[a-zA-Z0-9_.]+\s*,\s*\"([a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9-]*)\"')
flag_nonvar_re = re.compile(r'Flags\(\)\.(?!Get)(?:String|Bool|Int|Duration|Float64|Int64)(?:Slice|Array)?P?\(\s*\"([a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9-]*)\"')
diag_const_re = re.compile(r'\"(C[0-9]{3})\"')
cli_commands = set()
cli_flags = set()
for path in cli_files:
    try:
        with open(path) as fh: src = fh.read()
    except: continue
    for m in cmd_re.finditer(src): cli_commands.add(m.group(1))
    for m in flag_var_re.finditer(src): cli_flags.add(m.group(1))
    for m in flag_nonvar_re.finditer(src): cli_flags.add(m.group(1))
diagnostic_codes = set()
for path in diag_files:
    try:
        with open(path) as fh: src = fh.read()
    except: continue
    for m in diag_const_re.finditer(src): diagnostic_codes.add(m.group(1))
print(json.dumps({
    'cli_commands': sorted(cli_commands),
    'cli_flags': sorted(cli_flags),
    'diagnostic_codes': sorted(diagnostic_codes),
    'scan_skipped': ''
}))
"`
  output: code_surface

## build_manifest (v0.15.0): deterministic anchor extraction +
## verification + evidence collection. It bounds the campaign's
## semantic working set.
##
## For each doc in input.doc_files, scans the markdown and
## extracts code anchors via regex:
##   - file_ref      — paths like `pkg/foo/bar.go`, `docs/dsl.md`,
##                     `cmd/iterion/run.go:42` (with optional line
##                     suffix)
##   - cli_command   — backtick-quoted `iterion <subcmd>` invocations
##                     matching scan_code_surface.cli_commands
##   - cli_flag      — `--flag-name` mentions in inline code spans
##   - diagnostic    — `CNNN` codes referenced in prose
##   - symbol_ref    — backtick-quoted Go-style identifiers
##                     (`PackageName.TypeName` or `FunctionName`)
##                     that look like exported symbols
##
## Each anchor is then verified:
##   - file_ref     → does the file exist on disk?
##   - cli_command  → is it in input.cli_commands?
##   - cli_flag     → is it in input.cli_flags?
##   - diagnostic   → is it in input.diagnostic_codes?
##   - symbol_ref   → does `git grep -l` find it in code_scope_globs?
##
## Drift candidates = anchors whose verification failed. Each
## carries pre-fetched evidence (the doc line excerpt + the
## verifier's miss reason) so the campaign can judge semantics
## without re-enumerating the tree. Output is bounded:
## input.max_drift_candidates caps the candidate slice; the full
## counts ride along in the summary fields for the deterministic gate.
##
## All inputs flow via env vars (JSON-encoded for arrays); the
## tool's argv is empty.
tool build_manifest:
  command: `WS={{input.workspace_dir}} CODE_GLOBS={{input.code_scope_globs}} MAX_CANDIDATES={{input.max_drift_candidates}} MAX_CHUNK_DOCS={{input.max_review_chunk_docs}} python3 -c "
import os, json, re, glob, subprocess, sys
ws = os.environ['WS']
os.chdir(ws)
## List inputs ride argv after section markers (--docs/--cli/--flags/
## --diag): the engine substitutes an all-string list as space-joined
## escaped words — argv semantics. In an env assignment the second
## element would run as a command (exit 127). Same idiom as
## update_audit_cache's sys.argv tail.
buckets = {'--docs': [], '--cli': [], '--flags': [], '--diag': []}
cur = None
for a in sys.argv[1:]:
    if a in buckets:
        cur = a
        continue
    if cur is not None:
        buckets[cur].append(a)
doc_files = buckets['--docs']
cli_commands = set(buckets['--cli'])
cli_flags = set(buckets['--flags'])
diag_codes = set(buckets['--diag'])
code_globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('CODE_GLOBS', '').split(',') if g.strip()]
try:
    max_candidates = int(os.environ.get('MAX_CANDIDATES', '150'))
except ValueError:
    max_candidates = 150
try:
    max_chunk_docs = int(os.environ.get('MAX_CHUNK_DOCS', '0'))
except ValueError:
    max_chunk_docs = 0

## Regexes targeting markdown-typical patterns.
## file_ref: paths like pkg/foo/bar.go, docs/dsl.md, with optional :NN line.
## We restrict to known code/doc roots to avoid false hits on prose.
## (?<![./\w-]) instead of \b: \b matched the /→letter boundary INSIDE a
## longer path (e.g. '.archive/examples/foo.bot' captured 'examples/foo.bot',
## silently dropping the '.archive/' prefix → phantom file-not-found drift).
## The lookbehind refuses to start a match mid-path, so a known-root token is
## only captured when it's the true start of the reference.
FILE_RE = re.compile(r'(?<![./\w-])((?:pkg|cmd|internal|examples|docs|scripts|studio|.github|sdks)/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(?:go|md|ts|tsx|py|sh|yaml|yml|json|bot|iter))(?::([0-9]+(?:-[0-9]+)?))?')
## chr(96) = backtick. The iterion DSL uses backticks as the raw-string
## delimiter for tool commands, so any literal backtick inside the
## command body (e.g. an inline-code regex) terminates the string and
## breaks the parser. Building the pattern at runtime via chr() dodges
## the conflict without forcing a different command-string syntax.
BT = chr(96)
## cli_command: backtick-quoted 'iterion <subcmd>' (subcmd is 1st word after iterion)
CLI_CMD_RE = re.compile(BT + r'iterion\s+([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)')
## cli_flag: --kebab-flag tokens (allow leading backtick, allow trailing =)
CLI_FLAG_RE = re.compile(r'(?:\\s|^|' + BT + r'|\\()--([a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])\b')
## diag_code: literal CNNN with a 3-digit suffix.
DIAG_RE = re.compile(r'\b(C[0-9]{3})\b')
## symbol_ref: backtick-quoted CamelCase or pkg.Symbol identifiers.
## Strict pattern to avoid catching random capitalized words in prose.
SYM_RE = re.compile(BT + r'((?:[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\.)?[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\.[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]+)?)' + BT)

def excerpt(line_text, limit=140):
    s = line_text.strip()
    return s if len(s) <= limit else s[:limit] + '…'

## md_link (v0.16.x): internal markdown links [text](path#anchor).
## FILE_RE only matches prose paths under known roots and never the
## #anchor fragment, so a dead doc-to-doc link or a broken heading
## anchor (e.g. CLAUDE.md -> README.md#iter-vs-bot) slipped through the
## whole audit. This captures the link URL (first token after '(',
## stopping at whitespace so an optional "title" is ignored without
## needing a literal double-quote in this backtick command body) and
## verifies BOTH the target file's existence AND, for .md targets, the
## heading anchor — slugified GitHub-style. dead_link is already in the
## mismatch taxonomy; this feeds the campaign deterministic candidates.
MD_LINK_RE = re.compile(r'\[(?:[^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)')
_heading_cache = {}
def slugify(text):
    s = text.strip().lower().replace(BT, '')
    s = re.sub(r'[^\w\s-]', '', s)
    s = re.sub(r'\s', '-', s)
    return s.strip('-')
def headings_of(path):
    if path in _heading_cache:
        return _heading_cache[path]
    slugs = set()
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh:
            for ln in fh:
                hm = re.match(r'\s{0,3}(#{1,6})\s+(.+)', ln)
                if hm:
                    slugs.add(slugify(hm.group(2)))
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        pass
    _heading_cache[path] = slugs
    return slugs

anchors = []
per_doc_counts = {}
for doc in doc_files:
    try:
        with open(doc, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh:
            lines = fh.readlines()
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        per_doc_counts[doc] = {'total': 0, 'verified': 0}
        continue
    seen_in_doc = set()
    doc_anchors = []
    for lineno, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        for m in FILE_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            key = ('file_ref', value)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'file_ref', 'value': value, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
        for m in CLI_CMD_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            key = ('cli_command', value)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'cli_command', 'value': value, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
        for m in CLI_FLAG_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            key = ('cli_flag', value)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'cli_flag', 'value': value, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
        for m in DIAG_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            key = ('diagnostic', value)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'diagnostic', 'value': value, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
        for m in SYM_RE.finditer(line):
            value = m.group(1)
            ## Skip obvious markdown headings / English words. A heuristic:
            ## must contain at least one of [A-Z][a-z]+[A-Z] (CamelCase),
            ## OR a dot (pkg.Symbol).
            if not (re.search(r'[A-Z][a-z]+[A-Z]', value) or '.' in value):
                continue
            key = ('symbol_ref', value)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'symbol_ref', 'value': value, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
        for m in MD_LINK_RE.finditer(line):
            target = m.group(1)
            same_doc = target.startswith('#')
            if not same_doc and (re.match(r'^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://', target) or target.startswith(('mailto:', 'tel:', '//', 'www.'))):
                continue
            if '#' in target:
                path_part, frag = target.split('#', 1)
            else:
                path_part, frag = target, ''
            key = ('md_link', target)
            if key in seen_in_doc:
                continue
            seen_in_doc.add(key)
            doc_anchors.append({'doc': doc, 'line': lineno, 'kind': 'md_link', 'value': target, 'path_part': path_part, 'frag': frag, 'excerpt': excerpt(line)})
    anchors.extend(doc_anchors)
    per_doc_counts[doc] = {'total': len(doc_anchors), 'verified': 0}

## Verification pass.
## Symbol lookups are deferred to a single git grep batch — cheaper
## than O(N) shell calls. Build the union of distinct symbols first.
symbols_to_check = sorted({a['value'] for a in anchors if a['kind'] == 'symbol_ref'})
present_symbols = set()
if symbols_to_check and code_globs:
    ## git grep -l with alternation; chunk to avoid argv overflow.
    chunk_size = 100
    for i in range(0, len(symbols_to_check), chunk_size):
        chunk = symbols_to_check[i:i+chunk_size]
        pat = r'\b(' + '|'.join(re.escape(s.split('.')[-1]) for s in chunk) + r')\b'
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(['git', 'grep', '-lE', pat, '--'] + code_globs, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
            if r.returncode in (0, 1):
                ## We can only confirm at-least-one-match for the chunk
                ## as a whole; for per-symbol granularity we'd need
                ## N grep calls. Instead, do a content check: for each
                ## symbol in the chunk, regrep its leaf identifier in
                ## the union of matched files.
                matched_files = [l for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l]
                if matched_files:
                    for sym in chunk:
                        leaf = sym.split('.')[-1]
                        try:
                            rr = subprocess.run(['git', 'grep', '-lE', r'\b' + re.escape(leaf) + r'\b', '--'] + matched_files, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
                            if rr.returncode == 0 and rr.stdout.strip():
                                present_symbols.add(sym)
                        except Exception:
                            pass
        except Exception:
            pass

verified = []
drifted = []
unverifiable = []
for a in anchors:
    k = a['kind']
    v = a['value']
    if k == 'file_ref':
        if os.path.exists(v):
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'file exists'
            verified.append(a)
        else:
            a['status'] = 'drifted'
            a['evidence'] = 'file not found on disk'
            drifted.append(a)
    elif k == 'cli_command':
        ## When scan_code_surface was skipped/unscoped, cli_commands is
        ## empty -- we never scanned the CLI surface, so we cannot call a
        ## doc's iterion-subcommand reference drifted-from-code. Treat as
        ## unverifiable (excluded from the coverage denominator) rather
        ## than emitting a false drift for every existing command.
        if not cli_commands:
            a['status'] = 'unverifiable'
            a['evidence'] = 'cli surface not scanned (scan skipped / code_scope_globs unscoped) — cannot adjudicate'
            unverifiable.append(a)
        elif v in cli_commands:
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'present in scan_code_surface.cli_commands'
            verified.append(a)
        else:
            a['status'] = 'drifted'
            a['evidence'] = 'not in scan_code_surface.cli_commands'
            drifted.append(a)
    elif k == 'cli_flag':
        if not cli_flags:
            a['status'] = 'unverifiable'
            a['evidence'] = 'cli surface not scanned (scan skipped / code_scope_globs unscoped) — cannot adjudicate'
            unverifiable.append(a)
        elif v in cli_flags:
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'present in scan_code_surface.cli_flags'
            verified.append(a)
        else:
            a['status'] = 'drifted'
            a['evidence'] = 'not in scan_code_surface.cli_flags'
            drifted.append(a)
    elif k == 'diagnostic':
        if not diag_codes:
            a['status'] = 'unverifiable'
            a['evidence'] = 'diagnostic surface not scanned (diagnostic_surface_globs unset) — cannot adjudicate'
            unverifiable.append(a)
        elif v in diag_codes:
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'present in scan_code_surface.diagnostic_codes'
            verified.append(a)
        else:
            a['status'] = 'drifted'
            a['evidence'] = 'not in scan_code_surface.diagnostic_codes'
            drifted.append(a)
    elif k == 'symbol_ref':
        if v in present_symbols:
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'matched in code_scope_globs'
            verified.append(a)
        else:
            ## Symbol heuristic is brittle (CamelCase prose, archived
            ## examples, …); treat misses as unverifiable rather than
            ## drifted to avoid Goodhart-trap blockers.
            a['status'] = 'unverifiable'
            a['evidence'] = 'no match in code_scope_globs (may be prose or external ref)'
            unverifiable.append(a)
    elif k == 'md_link':
        ## Internal markdown link: verify the target file exists, and for
        ## a .md target verify the #heading-anchor too (line anchors #Lnn
        ## are code-line refs, not headings — skip those). High precision:
        ## os.path.exists + GitHub-slug heading match. Misses are real dead
        ## links (objective, verifiable), so they are 'drifted', not the
        ## softer 'unverifiable' used for the brittle symbol heuristic.
        path_part = a.get('path_part', '')
        frag = a.get('frag', '')
        tgt = a['doc'] if not path_part else os.path.normpath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(a['doc']), path_part))
        if path_part and not os.path.exists(tgt):
            a['status'] = 'drifted'
            a['evidence'] = 'link target not found: ' + tgt
            drifted.append(a)
        elif frag and not re.match(r'^[Ll][0-9]', frag) and tgt.endswith('.md'):
            if frag.lower() in headings_of(tgt):
                a['status'] = 'verified'
                a['evidence'] = 'anchor present in target'
                verified.append(a)
            else:
                a['status'] = 'drifted'
                a['evidence'] = 'dead anchor #' + frag + ' in ' + tgt
                drifted.append(a)
        else:
            a['status'] = 'verified'
            a['evidence'] = 'link target exists'
            verified.append(a)
    per_doc_counts[a['doc']]['verified'] += 1 if a['status'] == 'verified' else 0

## Prioritise candidates: drifted CLI surface first (highest signal),
## then drifted file_ref, then drifted diagnostic, then drifted symbol,
## then unverifiable symbols at the tail.
priority = {
    ('drifted', 'cli_command'): 0,
    ('drifted', 'cli_flag'): 0,
    ('drifted', 'md_link'): 1,
    ('drifted', 'diagnostic'): 1,
    ('drifted', 'file_ref'): 2,
    ('drifted', 'symbol_ref'): 3,
    ('unverifiable', 'symbol_ref'): 4,
}
## Surface drifted anchors plus unverifiable *symbol_ref*s (genuinely
## borderline — the campaign may resolve them by reading code). Do NOT
## surface scanner-skipped cli/flag/diagnostic unverifiables: we know
## we never scanned that surface, so there is nothing for the campaign to
## adjudicate — they would only be noise that tempts a false blocker.
candidates = drifted + [a for a in unverifiable if a['kind'] == 'symbol_ref']
candidates.sort(key=lambda a: (priority.get((a['status'], a['kind']), 9), a['doc'], a['line']))

## v0.16.0: doc-count chunking. After the severity sort, walk the
## ordered list and admit candidates from at most max_chunk_docs
## distinct docs. Once the doc budget is full, candidates from new
## docs are deferred (they surface in a later pass once the campaign
## clears earlier drifts). max_chunk_docs=0 disables chunking
## (preserves legacy candidate-cap-only behaviour).
docs_with_drift = sorted({a['doc'] for a in candidates})
if max_chunk_docs > 0 and len(docs_with_drift) > max_chunk_docs:
    chunk_docs = []
    chunked_candidates = []
    chunk_doc_set = set()
    for a in candidates:
        if a['doc'] in chunk_doc_set:
            chunked_candidates.append(a)
        elif len(chunk_doc_set) < max_chunk_docs:
            chunk_doc_set.add(a['doc'])
            chunk_docs.append(a['doc'])
            chunked_candidates.append(a)
    candidates = chunked_candidates
    chunked = True
else:
    chunked = False

if max_candidates > 0:
    candidates = candidates[:max_candidates]
chunk_doc_count = len({a['doc'] for a in candidates})

total_anchors = len(anchors)
verified_anchors = len(verified)
drifted_anchors = len(drifted)
unverifiable_anchors = len(unverifiable)
## Coverage is verified / (verified + drifted) — the fraction of
## MECHANICALLY-CHECKABLE anchors that verify. Unverifiable anchors
## (symbol-heuristic misses, and cli/flag/diagnostic anchors whose
## scanner was skipped/unscoped) are EXCLUDED from the denominator:
## counting them in the denominator dragged coverage below the gate
## every checkable claim was correct — e.g. 190 unverifiable / 907
## anchors capped achievable coverage at 79% < the 80% target, a
## permanent non-convergence deadlock. When nothing is checkable,
## coverage is vacuously complete (100) so the gate can still fire on
## campaign alignment instead of looping to exhaustion.
checkable_anchors = verified_anchors + drifted_anchors
coverage_pct = (verified_anchors * 100 // checkable_anchors) if checkable_anchors > 0 else 100

per_doc_list = sorted(
    [{'doc': d, 'total': c['total'], 'verified': c['verified']} for d, c in per_doc_counts.items()],
    key=lambda r: r['doc'],
)

print(json.dumps({
    'total_docs': len(doc_files),
    'total_anchors': total_anchors,
    'verified_anchors': verified_anchors,
    'drifted_anchors': drifted_anchors,
    'unverifiable_anchors': unverifiable_anchors,
    'checkable_anchors': checkable_anchors,
    'coverage_pct': coverage_pct,
    'drift_candidates': candidates,
    'per_doc_anchor_counts': per_doc_list,
    'all_audited_docs': sorted(doc_files),
    'docs_with_drift_count': len(docs_with_drift),
    'chunked': chunked,
    'chunk_doc_count': chunk_doc_count,
    'max_review_chunk_docs': max_chunk_docs,
    'verified_pairs': sorted({a['doc'] + '::' + str(a.get('value','')) for a in verified}),
}))
" --docs {{input.doc_files}} --cli {{input.cli_commands}} --flags {{input.cli_flags}} --diag {{input.diagnostic_codes}}`
  input: build_manifest_input
  output: build_manifest_output

agent author_docs:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-max}"
  input: author_input
  output: author_output
  system: author_docs_system
  user: author_docs_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## mark_issue_for_review (v0.15.4): after the run reaches a clean
## terminal state, transition the tracker issue out of `ready` so
## the dispatcher's polling loop doesn't re-dispatch it on the next
## tick (observed during run 019e4c2e: a no-op convergence walked
## the full workflow again on top of the previous commit, costing
## ~$1.50 per re-run for zero added signal).
##
## Skipped (no-op exit 0) when vars.issue_id is empty — manual CLI
## runs don't have a dispatcher-attached issue to transition.
##
## The store-dir is resolved relative to the worktree: the dispatcher
## puts the worktree at <host>/.iterion/dispatcher/workspaces/native_<id>/
## so three levels up lands on <host>/.iterion/ which is what
## `iterion issue move --store-dir` expects (the root that contains
## the `dispatcher/` subtree).
##
## v0.15.7: route through the dispatcher's HTTP API instead of
## the iterion CLI subprocess. The native store's in-memory index
## (pkg/dispatcher/native/store.go:s.index) is NOT invalidated
## when an external process writes the issue file — the dispatcher
## daemon's cached state survives the CLI's disk update and gets
## flushed back on the next Claim/Release, clobbering the move.
## Observed on the 2026-05-22 v0.15.6 dogfood (run 019e4e55):
## bot moved issue ready→review at 06:23:15, dispatcher's next
## Claim at 06:23:27 overwrote it back to ready without emitting
## an issue_state_changed event. The HTTP API path goes through
## the daemon's own Store instance, so the cache stays coherent.
##
## Port hardcoded to 4891 (the iterion studio default). If the
## studio is bound to a different port, override
## ITERION_DISPATCHER_PORT in the dispatcher's environment and
## tweak this command. Future iterion fix: expose dispatcher.port
## or dispatcher.url as a template var so this isn't hardcoded.
##
## ITERION_ISSUE_IDENTIFIER env var (set by the dispatcher per
## pkg/dispatcher/loop.go:450) carries the short issue id without
## the "native:" scheme prefix, which is exactly what the HTTP
## path needs: /api/v1/native/issues/{short-id}/transition.
##
## `|| true` keeps the workflow on the happy path if curl fails
## (network blip, daemon restart, etc.) — the commit has already
## landed.
tool mark_issue_for_review:
  command: `if [ -n {{vars.issue_id}} ]; then ID={{vars.issue_id}}; ID_SHORT=${ID#native:}; PORT=${ITERION_DISPATCHER_PORT:-4891}; curl -sS -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"to":"review"}' "http://localhost:${PORT}/api/v1/native/issues/${ID_SHORT}/transition" 2>&1 | head -c 1000 || true; else echo '{"skipped":"no issue_id"}'; fi`
  output: mark_issue_output

## update_audit_cache: rewrites the resolved audit_cache_path (absolute
## paths stay out-of-tree; relative paths resolve under workspace_dir)
## from the manifest's mechanically VERIFIED doc::anchor pairs (v2 —
## the cache no longer records reviewer claims). Each pair
## contributes a reference entry; the current sha1 of doc + refs is
## captured so the NEXT run's scan_docs can pre-verify untouched
## tuples. Also records the current git_head so the next run's
## scan_docs can short-circuit the whole campaign when HEAD is
## unchanged (the noop gate). Empty cache_path disables the write.
tool update_audit_cache:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, json, hashlib, re, sys, pathlib, subprocess
ws = sys.argv[1]
cache_path = sys.argv[2]
pairs = sys.argv[3:]
if not cache_path:
    print(json.dumps({'cache_path': '', 'entries_written': 0, 'git_head': '', 'skipped': 'no cache_path'}))
    sys.exit(0)
os.chdir(ws)
def git_head():
    try:
        return subprocess.run(['git', 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        return ''
def sha_of_file(p):
    try:
        with open(p, 'rb') as fh:
            return hashlib.sha1(fh.read()).hexdigest()
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError, PermissionError):
        return 'missing'
def extract_path(anchor):
    a = (anchor or '').strip()
    if not a or a.startswith('<'):
        return None
    m = re.match(r'([^\s:()]+)', a)
    return m.group(1) if m else None
by_doc = {}
for pair in pairs:
    if not isinstance(pair, str) or '::' not in pair:
        continue
    doc, anchor = pair.split('::', 1)
    p = extract_path(anchor)
    if not p:
        continue
    by_doc.setdefault(doc, set()).add(p)
doc_entries = {}
for doc, refs in by_doc.items():
    doc_entries[doc] = {
        'sha': sha_of_file(doc),
        'refs': sorted(({'p': p, 's': sha_of_file(p) if os.path.exists(p) else 'missing'} for p in refs), key=lambda r: r['p']),
    }
out_path = pathlib.Path(cache_path)
out_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
head = git_head()
with open(out_path, 'w') as out:
    json.dump({'v': 1, 'git_head': head, 'doc': doc_entries}, out, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
print(json.dumps({'cache_path': str(out_path), 'entries_written': len(doc_entries), 'git_head': head, 'skipped': ''}))
" {{input.workspace_dir}} {{input.cache_path}} {{input.audited_pairs}}`
  input: update_audit_cache_input
  output: update_audit_cache_output

## ─── Nodes : v2 campaign + gates ────────────────────────────────

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset so it works exactly like a native session: adjudicate the
## manifest's bounded drift set, verify at the anchor, fix the doc,
## commit each aligned doc in stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass
## re-derives its todo from the fresh manifest + `git log` (git is the
## durable state). Board capabilities power the findings handoff (code
## bugs found while verifying anchors → inbox), replacing v1's
## prepare_commit handoff.
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human
  capabilities: [board.read, board.create]

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). Kept FAST for docs-only passes.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-medium}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks
## (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def tree_state():
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
    except Exception:
        return None
pre = tree_state()
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
# Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
# deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
# CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
# must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
if rc == 0:
    import glob
    def has_drift_gate(text):
        for l in text.splitlines():
            if ('diff' in l and ('--exit-code' in l or '--quiet' in l)) or 'status --porcelain' in l:
                return True
        return False
    ci_hit = ''
    for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
        for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
            try:
                if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                    ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                    break
            except OSError:
                pass
        if ci_hit:
            break
    if ci_hit:
        try:
            vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
        except OSError:
            vs = ''
        if not has_drift_gate(vs):
            rc = 3
            out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
    if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
        post = tree_state()
        if post is not None:
            fresh = sorted(post - pre)
            if fresh:
                rc = 4
                out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## scope_check — the DETERMINISTIC writeable-set gate. Diffs the RUN
## BASE (oldest reflog entry of the fresh per-run worktree — exact
## under worktree: auto) against the working tree and flags any changed
## path that is not a doc: `.md` anywhere, the audit cache file, or
## (when GO_GLOBS is non-empty) a file matching those globs (the
## comment-only discipline stays contract-level, as in v1 — this gate
## is path-level containment). Untracked files are held to the same
## rule. No LLM; the campaign must revert whatever this flags.
tool scope_check:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} GO_GLOBS={{vars.go_comment_globs}} CACHE={{vars.audit_cache_path}} python3 -c "
import os, json, subprocess, fnmatch
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
go_globs = [g.strip() for g in os.environ.get('GO_GLOBS', '').split(',') if g.strip()]
cache = os.environ.get('CACHE', '').strip()

def git(args):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws] + args, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=120)
        return p.stdout or ''
    except Exception:
        return ''

reflog = [l for l in git(['reflog', '--format=%H']).splitlines() if l.strip()]
base = reflog[-1] if reflog else 'HEAD'
changed = set(l for l in git(['diff', base, '--name-only']).splitlines() if l.strip())
for line in git(['status', '--porcelain']).splitlines():
    if line[:2].strip() == '??':
        changed.add(line[3:].strip())

def allowed(p):
    if p.endswith('.md'):
        return True
    if cache and p == cache:
        return True
    for g in go_globs:
        if fnmatch.fnmatch(p, g):
            return True
    return False

out_of_scope = sorted(p for p in changed if not allowed(p))
log = ''
if out_of_scope:
    log = 'SCOPE VIOLATION: this run changed files outside the doc writeable-set (.md' + (' + go_comment_globs' if go_globs else '') + '). Revert these before continuing (git revert the offending commits, or git checkout ' + base[:12] + ' -- <path>): ' + ', '.join(out_of_scope[:40])
print(json.dumps({'scope_ok': not out_of_scope, 'out_of_scope': out_of_scope[:100], 'log': log}))
"`
  output: scope_check_output

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. converged = build
## green AND scope clean AND campaign reports docs aligned AND the
## manifest's mechanical anchor coverage meets the target. fail_log
## priority: scope violation (the campaign must revert) > build
## failure > empty (green-but-more-drift → the fresh manifest is the
## guidance). No LLM, no shell.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.scope_check.scope_ok && outputs.campaign.docs_aligned && outputs.build_manifest.coverage_pct >= vars.coverage_target_pct"
    fail_log: "if(!outputs.scope_check.scope_ok, outputs.scope_check.log, if(outputs.verify_run.passed, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail))"

## ─── Nodes : PR finalization tail (opt-in, feature-dev verbatim) ────

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output so the two outgoing
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"

## forge_auth_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-flight for the push. finalize_mr is an
## LLM agent; entering it with NO push credential mounted burns a whole turn
## just to rediscover, in shell, that it can't push. This tool answers "is
## there a push credential?" in ~100ms of subprocess so the graph only pays
## finalize_mr when a push can actually happen. python3 for safe JSON;
## read-only, idempotent.
tool forge_auth_probe:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
tok = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(tok) and os.path.getsize(tok) > 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'file:' + tok})); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    pass
for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
    if os.environ.get(v):
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'env:' + v})); raise SystemExit(0)
try:
    r = subprocess.run(['gh', 'auth', 'status'], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'gh host auth'})); raise SystemExit(0)
except Exception:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'available': False, 'reason': 'no forge_token secret, no *_TOKEN env, no gh host auth'}))
"`
  output: forge_auth_state

agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_DOC_ALIGN_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  tool_max_steps: 20

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow docs_refresh:
  entry: scan_docs

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/ —
  ## the campaign commits doc alignments in stride; the worktree +
  ## finalize FF keeps the operator's checkout untouched during the run
  ## and lands the series on a persistent branch on convergence. (v1 ran
  ## on the live tree with a terminal commit; in-stride commits make the
  ## worktree the safe default, aligned with the other v2 bots.)
  worktree: auto

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h"
    max_cost_usd: 60

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## DEFAULT-CREATE: if the repo has ZERO docs in scope, bootstrap an
  ## initial set first (author_docs), then loop back to scan_docs
  ## (author_rescan) so the re-scan picks the new docs up and the normal
  ## campaign polishes + commits them. author_rescan(1) = exactly one
  ## authoring pass; if it still produces nothing in scope the bounded
  ## loop fails loudly rather than spinning.
  ## NOOP short-circuit (openwiki-inspired): docs already aligned to the
  ## exact current HEAD, clean tree, no explicit request → skip the whole
  ## campaign. Highest-priority guard; mutually exclusive with no_docs
  ## (noop requires doc_count > 0) and the else fallback below.
  scan_docs -> done when noop_skip
  scan_docs -> author_docs when no_docs with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    docs_dir: "{{vars.docs_dir}}",
    recently_changed_code_files: "{{outputs.scan_docs.recently_changed_code_files}}"
  }
  scan_docs -> scan_code_surface else
  author_docs -> scan_docs as author_rescan(1)

  ## build_manifest runs after scan_code_surface (initial) AND as the
  ## continuation-loop head (gate re-entry), so the drift_candidates set
  ## the campaign sees always reflects the live worktree state. Both
  ## inbound edges supply identical with{} fields.
  scan_code_surface -> build_manifest with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    doc_files: "{{outputs.scan_docs.doc_files}}",
    cli_commands: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.cli_commands}}",
    cli_flags: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.cli_flags}}",
    diagnostic_codes: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.diagnostic_codes}}",
    code_scope_globs: "{{vars.code_scope_globs}}",
    max_drift_candidates: "{{vars.max_drift_candidates}}",
    max_review_chunk_docs: "{{vars.max_review_chunk_docs}}"
  }

  ## Each pass: the fresh manifest hands the campaign its bounded
  ## working set (+ the previous gate's feedback — empty on pass 1,
  ## outputs.gate resolves nil-safe before the gate has run).
  build_manifest -> campaign with {
    total_docs: "{{outputs.build_manifest.total_docs}}",
    total_anchors: "{{outputs.build_manifest.total_anchors}}",
    verified_anchors: "{{outputs.build_manifest.verified_anchors}}",
    drifted_anchors: "{{outputs.build_manifest.drifted_anchors}}",
    unverifiable_anchors: "{{outputs.build_manifest.unverifiable_anchors}}",
    manifest_coverage_pct: "{{outputs.build_manifest.coverage_pct}}",
    coverage_target_pct: "{{vars.coverage_target_pct}}",
    drift_candidates: "{{outputs.build_manifest.drift_candidates}}",
    docs_with_drift_count: "{{outputs.build_manifest.docs_with_drift_count}}",
    chunked: "{{outputs.build_manifest.chunked}}",
    chunk_doc_count: "{{outputs.build_manifest.chunk_doc_count}}",
    max_review_chunk_docs: "{{outputs.build_manifest.max_review_chunk_docs}}",
    recently_changed_code_files: "{{outputs.scan_docs.recently_changed_code_files}}",
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }

  ## Deterministic gates after every campaign pass: writeable-set
  ## containment, then the build gate, then the continuation decision.
  campaign -> scope_check
  scope_check -> verify_build
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged → transition the dispatcher issue (no-op without
  ## issue_id) and refresh the inter-run cache from the manifest's
  ## MECHANICALLY verified pairs, then finish.
  gate -> mark_issue_for_review when converged
  ## Not converged → another pass through the loop head: re-manifest
  ## (fresh drift set from the live tree) → campaign. A scope violation
  ## or build failure rides outputs.gate.fail_log on the
  ## build_manifest→campaign edge; green-but-more-drift rides an empty
  ## log — the fresh manifest is the guidance.
  gate -> build_manifest as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    doc_files: "{{outputs.scan_docs.doc_files}}",
    cli_commands: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.cli_commands}}",
    cli_flags: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.cli_flags}}",
    diagnostic_codes: "{{outputs.scan_code_surface.diagnostic_codes}}",
    code_scope_globs: "{{vars.code_scope_globs}}",
    max_drift_candidates: "{{vars.max_drift_candidates}}",
    max_review_chunk_docs: "{{vars.max_review_chunk_docs}}"
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while drift remains) →
  ## finish anyway, shipping what is banked (every committed alignment
  ## is real, landed work; the last campaign_output's drift_remaining
  ## tells the operator what is left).
  gate -> mark_issue_for_review

  mark_issue_for_review -> update_audit_cache with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    cache_path: "{{vars.audit_cache_path}}",
    audited_pairs: "{{outputs.build_manifest.verified_pairs}}"
  }
  ## ── PR tail (opt-in, feature-dev verbatim) ──────────────────────
  ## Reached on BOTH finishes (converged and max_passes-exhausted): the
  ## banked alignment commits are real landed work either way, and
  ## finalize_mr refuses an empty PR + reports drift_remaining honestly
  ## in the body. The noop_skip path (scan_docs -> done) never gets here.
  update_audit_cache -> mr_gate
  mr_gate -> forge_auth_probe when open_mr
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr

  forge_auth_probe -> finalize_mr when available with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}",
    campaign_summary: "{{outputs.campaign.summary}}",
    drift_remaining:  "{{outputs.campaign.drift_remaining}}"
  }
  forge_auth_probe -> done when not available
  finalize_mr -> done
